Title: EP07 — AI는 왜 헛소리를 하나
Credit: Written by
Author: vibecode.town
Series: Vibecode Town Season 1
Source: Concept episode — AI hallucination (evergreen/search)
Draft date: 2026-03-18
Duration: 3:30-4:00
Notes: v5 (6-segment format, narrator-only, 2D flat vector, Vee solo). High search volume concept. Target: "AI hallucination" "왜 AI가 거짓말"

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 1: HOOK [0:00-0:15]                                       */
/* emotion_curve: TRUST → BETRAYAL                                   */
/* narrative_stage: HOOK                                              */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: character_reaction | space: desk */

INT. VEE'S ROOM - NIGHT

= Vee reads AI output on her screen. It looks authoritative. Clean formatting. Bullet points. Citations. She nods along, taking notes.

/* vee_reaction: 1/6 */

= She googles one of the citations. The paper doesn't exist. She googles another. The author doesn't exist. She checks the URL. 404.

= Vee's pen stops mid-note. She stares at the screen. The AI's confident, well-formatted answer now looks different — like a con artist in a nice suit.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The answer was perfect. The formatting was flawless. The citations were invented. The AI lied to her face — and it looked absolutely certain while doing it.

> TITLE CARD: **AI는 왜 헛소리를 하나**

---

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 2: MISCONCEPTION [0:15-0:45]                              */
/* emotion_curve: FRUSTRATION → FALSE DIAGNOSIS                      */
/* narrative_stage: MISCONCEPTION                                    */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: diagram | space: whiteboard */

= A brain icon appears. A progress bar below it: "Knowledge: 73%." An arrow points up with the label "More Data."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The popular explanation goes like this: AI makes mistakes because it doesn't have enough data. Train it on more books, more websites, more code — and eventually it'll stop getting things wrong.

= The progress bar fills: 73% → 85% → 92% → 99%. The brain icon glows brighter.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
More training data. Bigger models. Better accuracy. Just keep feeding it information until it knows everything. Problem solved.

/* visual_type: character_reaction | space: desk */

/* vee_reaction: 2/6 */

= Vee nods along. Makes sense. More data, fewer mistakes. She types another question into the AI. The response is wrong again — a different kind of wrong. She frowns.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
GPT-4 was trained on most of the internet. It still makes things up. More data didn't fix it. Because data was never the problem.

---

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 3: THE_CRACK [0:45-1:15]                                  */
/* emotion_curve: FALSE DIAGNOSIS → UNSETTLING TRUTH                 */
/* narrative_stage: THE_CRACK                                        */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: diagram | space: whiteboard */

= The brain icon and progress bar shatter. Behind them — a simpler mechanism. A slot machine.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here's the part that makes people uncomfortable. AI isn't trying to be correct. It's not looking up answers. It's not reasoning. It's predicting the next word.

= The slot machine spins. Three reels. Each reel has words instead of symbols: "The" / "capital" / "of" — STOP. "France" / "is" / "Paris."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When it says "The capital of France is Paris" — it's not because it knows geography. It's because "Paris" is the most likely word to follow that sequence. It's pattern matching, not fact-checking.

= The slot machine spins again: "The" / "capital" / "of" — STOP. "Freedonia" / "is" / "Glendale."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And when the pattern is rare or ambiguous, it doesn't say "I don't know." It picks the most probable next word anyway. With full confidence. That's hallucination.

---

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 4: CORE [1:15-3:00]                                       */
/* emotion_curve: UNSETTLING TRUTH → UNDERSTANDING                   */
/* narrative_stage: CORE                                              */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: diagram | space: whiteboard */
/* extended_metaphor_start */

= TRANSITION: Clean white background. Center frame, a massive bookshelf appears. Thousands of books. A figure stands in front of it — not reading, but listening.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Imagine a person who has listened to every conversation ever recorded. Every book read aloud. Every podcast. Every lecture. Every argument.

= Sound waves flow into the figure's ears. Millions of them. The figure absorbs them all.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They've heard everything. But they've never checked any of it. They don't know what's true. They know what sounds right.

/* shorts_candidate */

= Someone asks the figure: "What year did Napoleon invade Russia?" The figure responds instantly, confident: "1812." Correct.

= Someone asks: "What year did Napoleon invade Japan?" The figure responds just as confidently: "1804." A red X appears. Japan never happened.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Same confidence. Same speed. One is right because the pattern is common. One is wrong because the pattern is rare — so the figure filled the gap with something that sounded plausible. It didn't hesitate. It didn't flag uncertainty. It just completed the sentence.

/* extended_metaphor_end */

/* visual_type: diagram | space: whiteboard */

= The bookshelf fades. Three technical concepts appear as simple diagrams:

= BOX 1: "Token Prediction" — a chain of words, each with a probability percentage. "The" → "cat" (42%) / "dog" (31%) / "president" (8%).

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Token prediction. Every word the AI writes is a probability gamble. It picks the most likely next word — or, if you turn up the temperature, it picks a less likely one for variety. That's the creativity dial. Turn it up, more creative. Turn it up too far, more nonsense.

= A temperature slider appears. Low: "safe, repetitive." Medium: "creative, useful." High: "unhinged, hallucinatory."

= BOX 2: "Context Window" — a horizontal bar, filled with text. As new text enters from the right, old text falls off the left edge.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Context window. The AI can only "see" a limited amount of text at once. Ask it about something from three thousand lines ago, and it's gone. It's not forgotten — it was never remembered. The AI is always reading through a keyhole.

/* vee_reaction: 3/6 */

= Quick flash: Vee at her desk. She measures the distance between her eyes and the screen with her hands — then puts her hands right up against her face, peeking through tiny gaps. Keyhole vision. 1.5 seconds.

= BOX 3: "No Truth Engine" — an empty box where a fact-checking module should be. A "MISSING" stamp over it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And the big one. There is no truth engine. No internal fact-checker. The AI has no mechanism to distinguish "this is correct" from "this sounds correct." It doesn't have beliefs. It doesn't have knowledge. It has statistics.

/* vee_reaction: 4/6 */

= Vee sits back in her chair. Arms crossed. Processing. This changes how she uses the tool.

/* visual_type: diagram | space: whiteboard */

= A split screen. Left: "More Data" with a red X. Right: "Better Verification" with a green check.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So more data doesn't fix hallucination. More data just gives the AI more patterns to mash together. The fix isn't a better AI. The fix is a better human.

/* shorts_candidate */

= The right side expands. Three verification methods appear: "Specs" (EP01 icon), "Tests" (EP04 icon), "Gates" (a new icon — a checkpoint barrier).

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Specs tell AI what the answer should look like. Tests confirm the answer is correct. Gates stop bad answers from shipping. The AI doesn't verify itself. You do.

---

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 5: REFRAME [3:00-3:30]                                    */
/* emotion_curve: UNDERSTANDING → ACCEPTANCE                         */
/* narrative_stage: REFRAME                                          */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: diagram | space: whiteboard */

= The slot machine from THE_CRACK reappears. But now it's reframed — labeled "CREATIVITY ENGINE."

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here's the twist nobody talks about. Hallucination and creativity are the same mechanism. The ability to generate something that doesn't exist yet — that's what makes AI useful AND unreliable. You can't have one without the other.

= The slot machine splits into two. Left: "Hallucination — wrong pattern completion." Right: "Creativity — novel pattern completion." Same machine, different outcome.

/* vee_reaction: 5/6 */

= Vee looks at the screen with a new expression — not frustration, not trust. Respect. Like she finally understands the tool she's using.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The fix isn't better AI. It's better systems around the AI. Let it be creative. Then verify everything.

---

/* ================================================================ */
/* SEGMENT 6: OUTRO_CTA [3:30-3:55]                                  */
/* emotion_curve: ACCEPTANCE → PRAGMATISM                            */
/* narrative_stage: OUTRO_CTA                                        */
/* ================================================================ */

/* visual_type: character_reaction | space: desk */

INT. VEE'S ROOM - NIGHT (CONTINUOUS)

= Vee at her desk. She reads an AI response. This time, she doesn't nod along. She highlights a claim. Opens a browser tab. Checks it. Correct. Highlights another. Checks. Wrong — she deletes it and writes her own version.

/* vee_reaction: 6/6 */

= Vee places a sticky note on her monitor: "AI는 자신감 있게 틀린다." (AI is confidently wrong.) She taps it once. Back to work.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
AI doesn't lie. It doesn't know what lying is. It completes patterns. Your job isn't to trust it or distrust it. Your job is to check.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Specs. Tests. Gates. That's the system. The AI creates. You verify.

> END CARD: 구독 + "다음 편: 바이브코더 5대 실수"

= FADE TO BLACK.

> END
